1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,300
You type a name, and a web page appears. But computers do not understand names.

2
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:07,403
Computers talk to numbers,

3
00:00:07,403 --> 00:00:09,434
the I P addresses you met earlier.

4
00:00:09,434 --> 00:00:12,122
Something has to turn the name into a number.

5
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:14,058
That something is D N S,

6
00:00:14,058 --> 00:00:17,984
and it is the first step of almost every connection on the internet.

7
00:00:18,534 --> 00:00:21,481
Today you trace one request from the name you type,

8
00:00:21,481 --> 00:00:23,792
all the way to the page that comes back,

9
00:00:23,792 --> 00:00:24,081
live.

10
00:00:26,131 --> 00:00:27,624
Why do we need names at all?

11
00:00:28,174 --> 00:00:30,969
People remember names. Computers need numbers.

12
00:00:31,519 --> 00:00:36,916
D N S is the phone book of the internet. You look up a name, and you get back an address.

13
00:00:37,466 --> 00:00:39,364
And because it is a separate step,

14
00:00:39,364 --> 00:00:41,207
a name can move to a new address,

15
00:00:41,207 --> 00:00:43,440
and nobody has to change what they type.

16
00:00:45,490 --> 00:00:49,309
Names are organised in a tree, and you read them from right to left.

17
00:00:49,859 --> 00:00:54,147
At the top is the root. Below it, the top-level names, like com and org.

18
00:00:54,697 --> 00:01:00,414
Below that, a company's name. And below that, the names inside it, like www.

19
00:01:00,964 --> 00:01:05,849
Each level is looked after by a different server. No single computer knows everything.

20
00:01:07,899 --> 00:01:11,974
A D N S answer is called a record, and there are a few types worth knowing.

21
00:01:12,524 --> 00:01:15,873
A record: a name points to an I P version four address.

22
00:01:16,423 --> 00:01:19,815
Quad A: the same, for an I P version six address.

23
00:01:20,365 --> 00:01:23,224
C name: this name is an alias for another name.

24
00:01:23,774 --> 00:01:26,270
M X: where the mail for this domain goes.

25
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:30,553
T X T: a note, often used to prove who owns the domain.

26
00:01:32,603 --> 00:01:34,246
Who actually does the looking up?

27
00:01:34,796 --> 00:01:39,510
Your computer asks a resolver, usually run by your network or your provider.

28
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:41,883
The resolver does the hard work,

29
00:01:41,883 --> 00:01:42,795
asking the root,

30
00:01:42,795 --> 00:01:43,877
then the top level,

31
00:01:43,877 --> 00:01:45,586
then the company's own server.

32
00:01:46,136 --> 00:01:51,234
Then it remembers the answer for a while. That time is called the T T L, in seconds.

33
00:01:51,784 --> 00:01:53,550
So the second lookup is fast.

34
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:55,315
And when you change a record,

35
00:01:55,315 --> 00:01:58,419
the old answer can linger until the T T L runs out.

36
00:02:00,469 --> 00:02:03,049
Let us look at a real lookup.

37
00:02:03,049 --> 00:02:03,583
First,

38
00:02:03,583 --> 00:02:05,985
who does this computer ask?

39
00:02:05,985 --> 00:02:08,298
Type: cat /etc/resolv.conf

40
00:02:08,428 --> 00:02:10,676
The nameserver line is the resolver.

41
00:02:10,676 --> 00:02:14,423
Here it is a small D N S server we started on this computer.

42
00:02:14,973 --> 00:02:22,034
Now ask it a question. Type: dig www.lab.test +noall +answer

43
00:02:22,164 --> 00:02:26,986
Each line is a record: the name, the time to live, the class, the type, and the value.

44
00:02:27,536 --> 00:02:33,317
Here www is an alias, a C name, for web. And web points to an address.

45
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:39,286
Now a different type. Type: dig lab.test MX +noall +answer

46
00:02:39,416 --> 00:02:43,576
The M X record: a priority number, ten, and the mail server's name.

47
00:02:44,126 --> 00:02:50,270
Last, a name that does not exist. Type: dig nope.lab.test | grep status

48
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:55,605
N X domain. It means: no such name. A typo in a name gives exactly this.

49
00:02:57,655 --> 00:03:01,218
Now put it all together. Here is what happens when you open a page.

50
00:03:01,768 --> 00:03:04,456
One: the name is looked up, and you get an address.

51
00:03:05,006 --> 00:03:09,422
Two: your computer opens a T C P connection to that address, on a port.

52
00:03:09,972 --> 00:03:16,158
Three: for H T T P S, the two sides run the T L S handshake, and check the certificate.

53
00:03:16,708 --> 00:03:19,652
Four: the browser sends an H T T P request.

54
00:03:20,202 --> 00:03:24,170
Five: the server sends a response, with a status code and a body.

55
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:27,750
Six steps, and every lesson so far is one of them.

56
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:37,885
Let us trace one request, end to end. First the name. Type: getent hosts www.lab.test

57
00:03:38,015 --> 00:03:41,820
This is the answer your computer's own resolver gets: the address,

58
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:43,434
and the names it belongs to.

59
00:03:43,984 --> 00:03:53,669
Now the request itself. Type: curl -s http://www.lab.test:8080

60
00:03:53,799 --> 00:03:58,066
The page comes back: hello from the lab. The name worked all the way through.

61
00:03:58,616 --> 00:04:08,728
One more look inside. Type: curl -sv www.lab.test:8080 2>&1 | grep -i connected

62
00:04:08,858 --> 00:04:10,151
Connected to the name,

63
00:04:10,151 --> 00:04:12,032
with the address it resolved to,

64
00:04:12,032 --> 00:04:12,796
and the port.

65
00:04:12,796 --> 00:04:13,443
Name first,

66
00:04:13,443 --> 00:04:14,383
then connection.

67
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:18,929
When something fails, pick the tool for the layer.

68
00:04:19,479 --> 00:04:22,082
Name problems: dig, or get ent hosts.

69
00:04:22,632 --> 00:04:25,384
Address and route problems: I P, and ping.

70
00:04:25,934 --> 00:04:28,984
Port and connection problems: s s, and curl.

71
00:04:29,534 --> 00:04:32,372
Web and certificate problems: curl dash v.

72
00:04:32,922 --> 00:04:35,567
Work from the name down, one layer at a time.

73
00:04:37,617 --> 00:04:39,238
Match the symptom to the layer.

74
00:04:39,788 --> 00:04:44,482
Could not resolve host: the name lookup failed. Check the name, and the resolver.

75
00:04:45,032 --> 00:04:48,530
N X domain: that name does not exist. Check the spelling.

76
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:52,686
Works by address, fails by name: D N S is the problem.

77
00:04:53,236 --> 00:04:57,310
Old address after a change: the T T L has not expired yet.

78
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:02,511
Name resolves, connection refused: the problem is the port, not D N S.

79
00:05:04,561 --> 00:05:05,990
Watch out for four myths.

80
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:10,316
Myth one: D N S is the internet. It is only the phone book.

81
00:05:10,866 --> 00:05:15,090
Myth two: a name has one address. It can have several, and it can change.

82
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:19,715
Myth three: changing a record is instant. It waits for the T T L.

83
00:05:20,265 --> 00:05:25,214
Myth four: if the page will not load, D N S is to blame. Check each layer first.

84
00:05:25,764 --> 00:05:27,108
Trace it, do not guess.

85
00:05:29,158 --> 00:05:31,483
Here is how you check your own understanding.

86
00:05:32,033 --> 00:05:35,105
Explain what D N S does, and why names exist.

87
00:05:35,655 --> 00:05:38,429
Name four record types and what each one is for.

88
00:05:38,979 --> 00:05:42,605
Describe the job of a resolver, and what the T T L does.

89
00:05:43,155 --> 00:05:46,441
Read a dig answer: name, T T L, type, value.

90
00:05:46,991 --> 00:05:49,807
List the steps from typing a name to seeing a page.

91
00:05:50,357 --> 00:05:53,109
Pick a tool for a name, a port, or a web problem.

92
00:05:53,659 --> 00:05:55,280
To pass, you need all six.

93
00:05:57,330 --> 00:05:59,613
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

94
00:06:00,163 --> 00:06:05,432
One: run cat /etc/resolv.conf, and find your resolver.

95
00:06:05,982 --> 00:06:09,459
Two: run dig on any public site, and read the answer line.

96
00:06:10,009 --> 00:06:13,593
Three: ask for its M X and its N S records, and compare.

97
00:06:14,143 --> 00:06:18,431
Four: run curl dash v on the same site, and find the connected line.

98
00:06:18,981 --> 00:06:21,904
Five: write the six steps of the journey in your own words.

99
00:06:22,454 --> 00:06:25,761
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

100
00:06:41,311 --> 00:06:42,186
Quick check.

101
00:06:42,736 --> 00:06:45,232
What does D N S do, and what is a resolver?

102
00:06:45,782 --> 00:06:47,339
Name three record types.

103
00:06:47,889 --> 00:06:49,745
What does the T T L control?

104
00:06:50,295 --> 00:06:52,066
What are the steps from name to page?

105
00:06:52,616 --> 00:06:56,754
Objective covered: MC03-O04.

106
00:06:57,304 --> 00:06:59,374
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

107
00:07:09,924 --> 00:07:13,252
